    "narrative": "For June 18, Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily into matchup exploitation to offset the current scarcity of stolen bases. **Jose Caballero** represents our strongest play today, boasting an elite 76.54 score. Facing Dakota Hudson, **Jose Caballero** benefits from a massive 13% platoon boost and, crucially, a slow pitcher delivery rating that maximizes his potential for swiped bags. Similarly, **Tyler Soderstrom** is primed for a breakout day with a 72.72 score; he faces a favorable matchup against a pitcher with an xERA of 5.29, and when coupled with his #4 spot in the order and a park factor boost, he is a locked-in core starter. While **Josh Naylor** isn't facing a basement-tier arm, his consistent form and presence in the #3 hole net him a solid 66.36, ensuring he remains a cornerstone of our daily production.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was leaving **Travis Bazzana** out of the lineup. Despite a respectable #1 spot in the order, his score of 37.03 fell just below our strict 40-point \"Zebras Floor.\" His performance was stifled by a difficult matchup against Drohan, where his L/L platoon disadvantage and the pitcher's sub-3.50 xERA forced us to prioritize the efficiency of **Brice Turang** and **Cole Young** to preserve our limited game caps. Elsewhere, we continue to monitor the status of **Trevor Story**, who remains sidelined on the IL, and we are managing the off-days for core assets like **Corbin Carroll** and **James Wood**, which naturally thins our positional depth today. We are playing for long-term category health, so we will keep **Mickey Moniak**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Austin Riley**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Max Muncy**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Carson Williams**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Kyle Karros**, **Alex Freeland**, and **Henry Bolte** on standby for late-inning pivots should weather or injury reports fluctuate.",
